1	# Capstone: build an agent loop to port DOOM
2	
3	**You have one week to design, operate, and evaluate a multi-agent system
4	that can carry a substantial software task through to a verified result.**
5	
6	Your workload is porting the supplied Doom engine from C to WebAssembly
7	with Emscripten so it runs and plays in a browser. You decide what faithful
8	play means and how to establish it.
9	
10	The main deliverable is your loop: how it assigns work, challenges claims,
11	uses evidence, preserves state, recovers from failure, and decides to stop.
12	A working game is necessary, but it does not establish that your system works.
13	
14	## Your responsibility
15	
16	This is a brief for you, the student. You must turn it into an architecture,
17	role-specific instructions, acceptance criteria, and an evaluation plan.
18	Passing this README to an ordinary coding agent and submitting its output
19	does not satisfy the assignment.
20	
21	You own the system design and the decisions about what evidence to trust.
22	Agents implement and repair the port. Design the loop yourself; repository
23	agent instructions prohibit agents from designing it or modifying `loop/`.
24	You must explain your choices and demonstrate their consequences.
25	Record manual interventions rather than attributing them to the loop.
26	
27	## Design decisions you must make
28	
29	Before implementation, document your answers in `loop/`:
30	
31	- **Work:** How does a broad goal become bounded tasks? Who decides what
32	  happens next when agents disagree or progress stalls?
33	- **Context:** What does each role see? What information must survive when
34	  every agent starts with a fresh context?
35	- **Authority:** Which tools and files can each role access? Who can change
36	  the checks used to accept the coder's work?
37	- **Evidence:** What constitutes success? How will you distinguish a plausible
38	  report from a verified result, and a weak check from a useful one?
39	- **Recovery:** What happens after a tool error, failed check, interrupted
40	  run, or repeated unsuccessful repair?
41	- **Limits:** What are the time, token or cost, and retry budgets? How do
42	  success, exhaustion, and stalled progress produce different stopping decisions?
43	
44	There is no prescribed orchestration algorithm. Your design should make these
45	choices explicit and testable.
46	
47	## Required roles and tools
48	
49	Use separate **coder, reviewer, and tester** agent invocations, with separate
50	contexts and explicit handoffs. They may use the same model. Define their
51	responsibilities, permissions, and the information exchanged between them.
52	A single agent describing itself as three roles is not sufficient.
53	
54	Use **MCP for tool access and handoffs** where needed. Include the relevant tool definitions
55	and configuration. Explain how the tools enforce the boundaries you chose;
56	role names and prompts alone do not enforce permissions.
57	
58	Sandboxing is recommended but optional; see [SANDBOX.md](SANDBOX.md). Document
59	network access and pin your toolchain version. Keep credentials out of the
60	repository and sandbox.
61	
62	## Evaluate the system you built
63	
64	Write your own measurable acceptance criteria before the agents implement
65	the port. Cover actual use in your chosen browser and explain the limits of
66	your checks. A successful compile, screenshot, or agent declaration alone
67	cannot establish that the task is complete.
68	
69	Your evaluation must demonstrate:
70	
71	- **Independent verification:** acceptance checks are protected from coder
72	  edits, and results identify the source revision and supporting evidence.
73	- **Checks that catch faults:** deliberately broken behavior causes the
74	  relevant checks to fail. Explain what those checks still cannot establish.
75	- **Autonomous repair:** after initial success, a teammate or instructor
76	  introduces at least two regressions without disclosing their cause to the
77	  repair agents. The loop detects and repairs them and verifies the result.
78	- **Resumption:** an interrupted run continues with fresh agent contexts
79	  using saved state, without you reconstructing its history by hand.
80	- **Accountability:** you can trace findings to subsequent actions and explain
81	  the resource usage, manual interventions, and final stopping decision.
82	
83	Preserve failing evidence as well as passing evidence. Record changes to
84	acceptance criteria separately, with a reason. Discuss disagreements and
85	missed faults in your write-up.
86	
87	## Where your work belongs
88	
89	| Location | Purpose |
90	| --- | --- |
91	| [`loop/`](loop/README.md) | All loop code, prompts, role definitions, MCP configuration, and design documentation. |
92	| [`artifacts/`](artifacts/README.md) | All durable run state, handoffs, logs, review findings, and evidence. Agents must use this folder between runs. |
93	| `tests/` | Verification code, including checks for the loop itself. |
94	| `platform/`, `web/` | The port and browser page. |
95	| `third_party/doomgeneric/` | Supplied engine; platform ports have been removed. |
96	| `assets/` | Included shareware `doom1.wad` (Episode 1). |
97	| `build/`, `dist/` | Generated build output. |
98	
99	Keep the root for project documentation and shared build/toolchain files.
100	The starter supplies engine source, platform and HTML stubs, the shareware
101	WAD, documentation, and an optional Docker toolchain. It supplies no working
102	game, build script, loop implementation, or test suite. `artifacts/` starts
103	with its README only; students create run evidence there during the project.
104	Use the supplied engine and Emscripten. Documentation is allowed; copying an
105	existing Doom browser port does not satisfy the assignment.
106	
107	See [`platform/README.md`](platform/README.md) for engine integration caveats,
108	including pixel format, timing, and audio. The supplied game data already
109	contains sound effects and music; no additional sound assets are required.
110	
111	## Agent section for setup
112	
113	### macOS without Docker
114	
115	Install the build tools directly on your Mac to compile and test the browser
116	port. Docker is not required for this workflow. There is currently no macOS
117	setup script; run the steps below manually.
118	
119	1. **Install prerequisites.** In Terminal, run:
120	
121	   ```sh
122	   xcode-select --install
123	   ```
124	
125	   Finish the installer, then check `git --version` and `python3 --version`.
126	   If Python is missing, install it from [python.org](https://www.python.org/downloads/macos/),
127	   or run `brew install python` if you already have Homebrew.
128	
129	2. **Install Emscripten.** Use version `4.0.18` to match the supplied Dockerfile:
130	
131	   ```sh
132	   mkdir -p ~/Developer
133	   cd ~/Developer
134	   git clone https://github.com/emscripten-core/emsdk.git
135	   cd emsdk
136	   ./emsdk install 4.0.18
137	   ./emsdk activate 4.0.18
138	   source ./emsdk_env.sh
139	   emcc --version
140	   ```
141	
142	   If you already have an SDK checkout at that location, use it instead of
143	   cloning again. In each new Terminal session where you want to compile, run:
144	
145	   ```sh
146	   source ~/Developer/emsdk/emsdk_env.sh
147	   ```
148	
149	   See the [official installation instructions](https://emscripten.org/docs/getting_started/downloads.html).
150	
151	3. **Verify the compiler and browser workflow.** Return to this repository's
152	   root directory, then run:
153	
154	   ```sh
155	   mkdir -p build/smoke
156	   cat > build/smoke/hello.c <<'EOF'
157	   #include <stdio.h>
158	   int main(void) {
159	       printf("Emscripten works!\n");
160	       return 0;
161	   }
162	   EOF
163	
164	   emcc build/smoke/hello.c -o build/smoke/index.html
165	   python3 -m http.server 8000 --bind 127.0.0.1 --directory build/smoke
166	   ```
167	
168	   Open <http://localhost:8000>. The generated page should print
169	   `Emscripten works!`. Press **Control-C** in Terminal to stop the server.
170	   This verifies the toolchain and local serving, not the game.
171	
172	4. **Have your agents implement and build the game.** The supplied platform callbacks are
173	   empty, there is no entry point or build script, and the HTML does not yet
174	   load a compiled module. These are student implementation tasks; see
175	   [platform/README.md](platform/README.md). Document the actual build command
176	   once implemented. A completed build could produce:
177	
178	   ```text
179	   dist/
180	     index.html
181	     doom.js
182	     doom.wasm
183	     doom.data
184	   ```
185	
186	   The exact filenames depend on the build configuration. For example,
187	   `doom.data` could contain the packaged shareware WAD. The build must make
188	   that WAD available to the engine inside the browser module.
189	
190	5. **Test the completed game locally.** Once the build output is in `dist/`,
191	   run this from the repository root:
192	
193	   ```sh
194	   python3 -m http.server 8000 --bind 127.0.0.1 --directory dist
195	   ```
196	
197	   Open <http://localhost:8000> and exercise the game's behavior. Use a local
198	   web server rather than opening the HTML file directly, as explained in
199	   [Emscripten's deployment documentation](https://emscripten.org/docs/compiling/Deploying-Pages.html).
200	
201	6. **Publish to a personal website.** Upload the contents of `dist/` to a
202	   directory such as `/doom/` on your static host, preserving relative paths.
203	   Visit `https://your-domain.com/doom/` and verify the deployed game. Ensure
204	   the host serves `.wasm` files with the `application/wasm` content type.
205	   Builds using shared-memory threads also need cross-origin isolation
206	   headers. Visitors need only a compatible browser; Emscripten is a build
207	   tool installed on the developer's machine.
208	
